From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] drm/mediatek: mtk_dsi: add MT6897 and its two links

Add the DSI of MT6897, with what its host needs that the others do not:

- It computes the D-PHY timing and the marks of its TX buffer from the
  nominal rate of the panel, the same as the vendor, so a separate
  value.
- The horizontal porches are in whole words, and the sync pulse of the
  front porch has its own field, from mtk_dsi_calc_vdo_timing().
- Its TX buffer has marks for the bus priority (mtk_dsi_tx_buf_rw()).
  The high SODI mark depends on the rate of the engine clock.
- It sends a command in the vertical front porch without stopping the
  video, which dimming the panel uses. A command that does not fit, or a
  read, stops the video. The last commands before a panel goes off go
  without video.

This host polls like the vendor's does: for BUSY after a command, and
for the end of a frame and then BUSY to stop the video. Its interrupt
stays off. The interrupt handler spins on BUSY, which stays set with the
video running, so a completion in that time would never return.

A panel on two links has one host for each half of the screen, as this
SoC has no DSC for two links at once. The panel attaches to both. The
CRTC tells the first one which one its second link is, because it has
both in its path. From there the first link runs both of them:

- The second link starts along with the first through DSI_DUAL_EN.
- The commands go out on both links at the same time, and a read on the
  first link only. The second link refuses a transfer. The panel driver
  sends everything to the first.
- The porches and the widths are for one link, and the data rate is what
  the panel asks for (dsi->hs_rate). Other hosts keep their own rate.

The registers only answer while the block has power and clocks, and an
access otherwise stops the bus. So this host takes its own power domain
when it powers on, as the CRTC may have failed to get it. A transfer
without both links on fails: the backlight of the panel comes here too,
even with the display going off, so a lock keeps it apart from the links
going on and off.

A host that the bootloader left running stops at the end of a frame
before anything else changes. The other hosts are as they were.

This is not tested on a device. The order and the values are from the
vendor driver, and a register model in QEMU has been through it.
